WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.900
Begin with the flower overview.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.267
A labels the stigma at the top of the central carpel, and H points to the

00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:07.580
style

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.060
below it.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.360
B identifies an anther, with C indicating its supporting filament.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.120
Anther and filament together form a stamen.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.500
D points to a petal and E to a sepal.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.640
The drawing shows selected organs in a schematic longitudinal view, not the

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.500
exact number or proportion found in a particular species.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.460
Trace leader lines to their endpoints before naming a structure.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.720
Do not confuse the filament at C with the central style at H merely because

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.400
both are slender.

00:00:36.864 --> 00:00:38.724
Now compare F and G.

00:00:39.404 --> 00:00:43.684
F points to the wall of the ovary, while G points to an ovule inside it.

00:00:44.764 --> 00:00:49.024
Following normal seed development, an ovule becomes a seed and the ovary

00:00:49.024 --> 00:00:53.364
usually develops into a fruit, sometimes with contributions from other floral

00:00:53.364 --> 00:00:53.784
tissues.

00:00:55.784 --> 00:00:57.564
They are not interchangeable labels.

00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:01.644
D and E identify a petal and a sepal respectively.

00:01:02.284 --> 00:01:07.544
The stigma receives pollen; fertilisation occurs later after the appropriate

00:01:07.544 --> 00:01:08.804
events within the ovule.

00:01:09.784 --> 00:01:12.924
A pollen grain on the stigma is therefore not already a seed.

00:01:13.904 --> 00:01:18.024
Keep the organ, its internal structure and its developmental fate as separate

00:01:18.024 --> 00:01:18.964
parts of the explanation.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.840
The placentation figure has three panels.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.360
A is a longitudinally opened ovary with ovules attached along one margin,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.440
illustrating marginal placentation.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.860
B is a transverse section with partitions meeting at a central axis where

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.880
ovules attach: this is axile placentation.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.860
C is also a transverse section, but the ovules attach to the inner peripheral

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.140
wall in the illustrated parietal pattern.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.840
Read the section labels L S and T S before comparing the panels.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:54.060
Different views can reveal different features, so do not treat the elongated

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.460
outline in A as evidence that it belongs to a completely different organ.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.760
The shared question is where the ovules attach.

00:02:00.984 --> 00:02:03.184
Look again at B and C.

00:02:03.924 --> 00:02:08.204
B contains partitions, or septa, extending from the wall toward the central

00:02:08.204 --> 00:02:08.544
axis.

00:02:09.424 --> 00:02:11.284
This supports the axile interpretation.

00:02:12.224 --> 00:02:15.886
A centrally positioned placenta without these partitions can instead

00:02:15.886 --> 00:02:16.424
illustrate

00:02:16.424 --> 00:02:20.164
a free central pattern, which is not drawn as a separate panel here.

00:02:20.924 --> 00:02:25.224
In C, ovules are peripheral rather than attached to the illustrated central

00:02:25.224 --> 00:02:25.644
axis.

00:02:26.444 --> 00:02:31.184
Real ovaries may have additional features, including false partitions, so a

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:35.344
simple diagram is a teaching model rather than a universal species diagnosis.

00:02:36.444 --> 00:02:40.024
Name the attachment and supporting structures you can actually see before

00:02:40.024 --> 00:02:41.404
applying a memorised example.

00:02:42.324 --> 00:02:45.304
Letter B does not always mean axile in another question.

00:02:45.552 --> 00:02:48.892
The plant tissue comparison shows schematic cell sections.

00:02:49.592 --> 00:02:53.652
A has relatively thin walls, represented nuclei and intercellular spaces,

00:02:54.392 --> 00:02:55.392
fitting typical parenchyma.

00:02:56.272 --> 00:03:00.332
B shows living cells with uneven thickening at the corners, the familiar

00:03:00.332 --> 00:03:01.212
collenchyma pattern.

00:03:02.012 --> 00:03:06.412
C shows thick walls around narrow lumens, with no living protoplast

00:03:06.412 --> 00:03:08.332
represented, fitting mature sclerenchyma.

00:03:08.892 --> 00:03:11.912
The clear central spaces in C are not nuclei.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:16.112
Wall chemistry and developmental context are additional evidence in a real

00:03:16.112 --> 00:03:19.792
specimen; a cartoon does not perform a chemical test for lignin.

00:03:20.712 --> 00:03:24.732
Use the combination of living state, wall distribution and lumen rather than

00:03:24.732 --> 00:03:26.612
calling every thick walled cell sclerenchyma.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.360
Keep the same tissue panels and add function carefully.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.100
Parenchyma at A can support storage or metabolism, and some parenchyma cells

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.360
photosynthesise.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.320
A thin walled cell in a schematic does not by itself prove that it contains

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:42.220
chloroplasts.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.760
Collenchyma at B supplies flexible support in familiar growing organs.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.088
Mature sclerenchyma at C typically provides strong support through thick,

00:03:52.088 --> 00:03:52.360
often

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:55.280
lignified walls after loss of the living protoplast.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.940
These are standard functional comparisons, not claims that each tissue has

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.420
exactly one role everywhere.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.835
State which observed structural clue supports the function and what

00:04:05.835 --> 00:04:06.340
information

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.580
the drawing does not provide.

00:04:07.872 --> 00:04:11.132
The animal tissue figure uses three simplified views.

00:04:11.952 --> 00:04:15.412
A shows a single layer of flattened epithelial cells on a basement membrane,

00:04:15.792 --> 00:04:17.872
fitting simple squamous epithelium.

00:04:18.552 --> 00:04:22.894
B shows cells distributed among fibres in an extracellular matrix, a

00:04:22.894 --> 00:04:23.532
connective

00:04:23.532 --> 00:04:24.112
tissue pattern.

00:04:25.052 --> 00:04:29.446
C shows parallel striated skeletal muscle fibres with several peripheral

00:04:29.446 --> 00:04:29.812
nuclei

00:04:29.812 --> 00:04:30.372
represented.

00:04:31.352 --> 00:04:34.852
Do not treat the different panel heights as measured tissue thicknesses,

00:04:34.852 --> 00:04:38.452
because the drawings use different viewing orientations and arbitrary scales.

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:43.772
A tissue identification should use arrangement, matrix, cell form and other

00:04:43.772 --> 00:04:44.772
relevant clues together.

00:04:45.332 --> 00:04:48.861
A round mark in one image can be a nucleus or a sectioned structure; its

00:04:48.861 --> 00:04:49.080
context matters.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.540
Finish with C.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:55.986
Striations support a striated muscle identification, but cardiac muscle is

00:04:55.986 --> 00:04:56.220
also

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:56.620
striated.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.547
The parallel, unbranched fibres and multiple peripheral nuclei represented

00:05:01.547 --> 00:05:01.760
here

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.880
support the skeletal muscle interpretation.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:09.460
A small section or poor image can hide branching, nuclei or junctions, so a

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.780
single stripe is not a complete diagnostic key.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:16.880
A at the top is an epithelial lining rather than a muscle layer, and B

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:22.100
emphasises extracellular matrix rather than parallel contractile fibres.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:26.078
Explain why your chosen answer fits several features, then state a limit of

00:05:26.078 --> 00:05:26.820
the schematic.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:32.523
These figures teach structural recognition and do not identify a disease from

00:05:32.523 --> 00:05:32.580
a

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:32.940
tissue image.
